KW  - Large language models
KW  - Membership inference attacks
KW  - Machine learning
KW  - Model security and privacy
AB  - Membership inference attacks (MIAs) compromise model privacy by determining whether a given sample belongs to the training dataset of a target model. Current membership inference attacks primarily operate by exploiting the outputs of the target model (reference-free MIAs) or by comparing the outputs of the target model and the reference model (reference-based MIAs). Although reference-based MIAs typically achieve higher attack performance, such methods generally require access to a dataset with a distribution similar to that of the target model’s training set, which is often infeasible in practical scenarios. Furthermore, fine-tuning the reference model requires additional computational resources. This paper proposes a Shortened Prefixes-based Weighted Membership Inference Attack (SPW-MIA). Driven by our consideration of memorization in large language models (LLMs), we propose a method that dynamically shortens the initial prefix of tokens and compares the target model’s prediction differences for the corresponding tokens under the initial prefix and the shortened prefix, thereby assigning weights to the tokens. Furthermore, based on intuition, we incorporate the difference between the probabilities of the tokens greedily generated by the target model and the probabilities of the tokens in the sample into the final judgment. Similar to other MIAs, our attack requires access to the target model’s outputs but operates without the reference model. We evaluate our method on multiple LLMs fine-tuned on different datasets as well as on several pre-trained LLMs, and compare it with several reference-free MIAs. The results demonstrate that our method achieves significant attack performance.
