AB  - Large language model (LLM) serving at the edge is constrained by limited memory and compute, while cloud offloading can expose privacy-sensitive data. These constraints motivate edge–cloud split inference, where front layers run at the edge and the remaining layers run on cloud servers. Existing systems such as EdgeShard primarily formulate this trade-off as a model partitioning problem. However, prefill remains a major bottleneck: long prompts require substantial edge and cloud computation and large activation transfers, making latency depend not only on layer placement but also on coordination under variable network conditions. We therefore reformulate edge–cloud LLM serving as network-paced prefill orchestration. TandemServe orchestrates prefill as a network-paced stream of activation flows, overlapping edge computation, transfer, and cloud computation while preventing long prefill transfers from interfering with latency-sensitive decode traffic. Evaluated on a real edge–cloud platform using a vLLM-based runtime, TandemServe reduces mean TTFT and TPOT by geometric means of 3.1× and 1.75×, respectively, over an EdgeShard-style baseline on the same runtime, while improving output-token throughput by 1.26×.
