AB  - Long-term forecasting of servo-motor signals is fundamental to predictive maintenance in battery swapping stations to prevent severe downtime. SegRNN is a relevant reference model for industrial long-horizon forecasting due to its competitive accuracy and memory costs, while LLMs offer a promising means of transforming forecasting results into actionable maintenance insights. However, SegRNN may still suffer from long-term memory degradation over extended horizons, and LLM-based reasoning is often constrained by static or insufficiently governed knowledge under evolving maintenance standards. To address these challenges, a long-term memory-enhanced forecasting framework is proposed, which combines robust numerical forecasting with expert-guided semantic reasoning. A time-frequency residual memory module is introduced to preserve multi-scale temporal patterns and alleviate long-horizon forgetting. In addition, a human-in-the-loop mechanism enables LLMs to dynamically refine the external knowledge base through expert feedback, improving explanation consistency and traceability. Experiments on a private real-world dataset—comprising over one million shuttle car servo-motor data points and 500 + fault reports—demonstrate that at a 720-step horizon, the proposed method reduces torque and speed RMSE by 0.1255 and 0.3486, respectively, compared to SegRNN. Additionally, the proposed HLME mechanism based on Qwen3 improves the ROUGE-L score of generated explanations by 0.137 over the RAG baseline.
