AB  - To systematically clarify the technical landscape and theoretical-practical value of code semantic alignment, this paper conducts a comprehensive survey of 113 high-quality studies published between 2023 and 2025. Centering on ŉeural network-driven code semantic alignment optimization’, we propose a three-perspective classification framework of ’Contrastive Learning (discriminative representation) + Structural Modeling (logical capture) + LLM Collaboration (semantic enhancement)’, and systematically dissect the evolutionary context and core optimization logic of 12 technical branches-including hard negative sample mining, multi-view learning, AST-based GNN embedding, multigraph fusion, and LLM-aided query expansion. A three-dimensional evaluation system integrating ’effectiveness-efficiency-robustness’ is established, supplemented by software engineering-specific indicators (compatibility relevance, maintainability alignment), to quantitatively benchmark 15 representative methods on mainstream datasets (CodeSearchNet, CoSQA+, smart contract datasets). Furthermore, we deeply analyze core challenges at both technical and application levels: low-resource adaptation bottlenecks in specialized domains, cross-scenario (cross-domain/cross-lingual) semantic transfer barriers, the triangular balance dilemma of accuracy-efficiency-interpretability, and the disconnection between academic research and industrial toolchain integration. Finally, future research directions are prospected from six aspects: deep synergy of three-perspective methods, LLM-task-specific model collaboration, dynamic semantic modeling, interpretable alignment enhancement, low-resource/cross-scenario optimization, and industrial-grade toolchain adaptation. A thematic and chronological analysis of the 113 studies reveals the field’s evolution from ’independent technical development’ to ’deep integration of Contrastive Learning and Structural Modeling’. This paper clarifies the technical logic and research hotspots of code semantic alignment, providing a systematic reference for researchers and engineers to bridge theoretical innovations and practical industrial applications.
