AB  - We conduct a systematic review of ethical, technical, and governance challenges in the development and deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) and map these challenges to machine-learning (ML) strategies and governance practices reported in recent research. Using a PRISMA-guided process, we identified 1300 records, removed 220 duplicates (1080 unique), screened titles/abstracts, assessed 480 full texts, and included 101 studies (2021–2025) spanning healthcare, education, law, and public policy. From this corpus, we synthesize a taxonomy covering data curation, consent and privacy, bias representation, provenance, and auditability, and align each area with actionable ML controls (e.g., documentation and tracing, bias detection and mitigation, privacy-preserving learning, quality checks, and auditing). Building on this synthesis, we present an integrative governance framework that links dataset processes and ML controls to oversight checkpoints across the LLM lifecycle, supporting accountable development and deployment. Overall, the review provides an evidence-based map from ethical risks to implementable ML techniques and governance touchpoints to reduce harm and strengthen transparency and accountability in real-world LLM pipelines.
