AB  - This chapter provides an overview of the integration of generative artificial intelligence (AI) and Large Language Models (LLMs) into Digital Twin-empowered smart grids. We first introduce the shape of the future energy system, with special emphasis on the role of software-defined microgrids (SDMG) in enabling real-time, flexible energy coordination. The chapter then moves to introduce the Digital Twin concept, empowering virtual replicas of physical grid assets to enable monitoring, predictive analytics, and decision-making. The focus is on the use of LLMs for smart grid operations to offer semantic interoperability, improve data processing from heterogeneous sources, and offer intelligent control and forecasting. The chapter stresses the use of LLMs for domain-specific tasks using methods such as Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) and how LLMs can be used for applications such as predictive maintenance, fault detection, and load forecasting. Furthermore, the application of generative-AI techniques such as variational autoencoders (VAEs), conditional GANs (cGANs), and reinforcement learning is addressed for battery condition monitoring and system-wide planning for contingency. These approaches leverage spatio-temporal modeling, graph neural networks (GNNs), and hybrid AI strategies to support grid-wide situational awareness and adaptive control. Practical implementation strategies are outlined, including architecture designs, optimization frameworks, and edge AI considerations. A unique synthesis of LLMs, generative-AI, and Digital Twins has been discussed for forming a unified framework for smart grid analysis and control. Practical implementation strategies have been presented, including architecture design, optimization methods, and edge-AI deployment. Several future directions have also been discussed, including Digital-Twin-in-the-Loop (DTiL) validation, federated and privacy-preserving learning, semantic standardization, and scalable orchestration of SDMG. Overall, the chapter furnishes a systematic overview of how the confluence of generative-AI, LLMs, and Digital Twins can significantly enhance the intelligence, adaptability, and efficiency of next-generation intelligent grid infrastructures.
