AB  - Opioid-induced respiratory depression (OIRD) is a serious postoperative complication that is difficult to predict due to the heterogeneity of clinical risk factors and the prevalence of unstructured documentation. This study presents a comprehensive benchmarking framework for the prediction of OIRD risk using a retrospective clinical dataset, collected at Hamad Medical Corporation, comprising 3401 patient records from bariatric surgery. We compare fine-tuned transformer encoder models (ClinicalBERT, BioBERT, and MedEmbed), large language models (LLMs) operating in zero-shot and few-shot settings, and hybrid NLP-machine learning approaches integrating structured variables with text-derived features, on this dataset. In the zero-shot setting, GPT-4 achieved an accuracy of 0.58 with high sensitivity (0.92) but low specificity (0.22), yielding an F1-score of 0.69 and an AUROC of 0.63; few-shot prompting preserved accuracy and sensitivity while modestly improving specificity (0.25), F1-score (0.68), and AUROC (0.68). Similarly, Gemini demonstrated slightly higher zero-shot accuracy (0.61) and sensitivity (0.93), with limited specificity (0.27), an F1-score of 0.71, and an AUROC of 0.62; few-shot prompting led to marginal gains in accuracy (0.62), specificity (0.28), F1-score (0.70), and AUROC (0.67), while maintaining high sensitivity. The Encoder models exhibit considerable sensitivity and proficient extraction of clinically relevant signals from narrative data, however, their discriminative efficacy is hindered by the scarcity of fine-tuning data. Hybrid NLP-machine learning models that utilize TF-IDF and dimensionality reduction achieve superior predictive performance, whilst LLMs demonstrate continuously elevated sensitivity but decreased specificity in prompt-based inference.
