AB  - Public transit infrastructure serves as a critical determinant of urban equity, yet evaluations of transit disparity historically rely on macroscopic proximity metrics, overlooking the micro-scale quality of the transit node environment. To address this methodological gap, this study introduces a scalable, automated framework utilizing Vision-Language Models (VLMs) for high-resolution transit stop assessment. We develop the Bus Stop Boarding Environment Index (BSBEI), a multidimensional metric encompassing six environmental domains. Leveraging zero-shot visual instruction tuning, our framework autonomously audited approximately 115,000 multi-perspective street-view panoramas across five diverse metropolitan regions (Seoul, Wellington, Santa Clara, Chicago, and London). Our analysis reveals a pervasive bimodal distribution of infrastructure quality dominated by a low-score peak, suggesting that transit agencies across these distinct contexts default to a minimum viable operational paradigm that restricts premium amenities. Spatial stratification analysis utilizing Lorenz curves and Gini coefficients indicates significant disparities, particularly within the accessibility and information sub-dimensions. To elucidate the geographic and socioeconomic drivers of these inequities, we applied geospatial analysis and econometric models, including Local Indicators of Spatial Association (LISA), quantile regression and group comparison tests (ANOVA and Kruskal–Wallis). Correlating BSBEI scores with covariates, e.g., ridership intensity, latent demand, neighborhood deprivation, and urban typology, we identified a consistent positive association between environmental quality and actual ridership. Based on these drivers, we classify the transit systems into four distinct archetypes: demand-driven (Chicago), development-oriented (Seoul), spatially heterogeneous (Wellington and Santa Clara), and highly standardized (Central London). By successfully integrating VLMs with public transit evaluation, this research validates the feasibility of large-scale, automated micro-environment auditing, providing a robust empirical foundation for targeted policy interventions and the optimization of global transit equity.
