AB  - Privacy-preserving and explainable machine learning for fetal health prediction is essential for trustworthy clinical decision support under strict medical data regulations. This study proposes a unified framework for fetal health classification from cardiotocography (CTG) data, integrating synthetic data generation, a transformer-based feature interaction module, federated learning, differential privacy, and explainable artificial intelligence. To address data scarcity and class imbalance, a hybrid synthetic data pipeline is developed using variational autoencoders (VAEs), conditional tabular GANs (CTGANs), and a large language model (LLM)-based generation. To improve structured data modeling, an Inter-Feature Attention Transformer (IFAT) is introduced that treats each clinical variable as a token and captures inter-feature dependencies via self-attention. In decentralized healthcare settings, federated learning enables collaborative training without sharing raw data and is evaluated under IID and non-IID settings to study statistical heterogeneity in clinical distributions. Differentially Private Stochastic Gradient Descent (DP-SGD) is employed during optimization to provide formal privacy guarantees (ϵ≈3.0, δ=10−5), maintaining 92%–93% classification accuracy while preserving the privacy of individual training samples. SHAP-based explainability ensures interpretability across centralized, federated, and privacy-preserving settings, consistently identifying fetal heart rate, short-term variability, and accelerations as key predictors. The proposed framework exhibits linear computational complexity with respect to dataset size, feature dimension, model parameters, and the number of participating clients, making it suitable for large-scale privacy-preserving healthcare deployments. Experimental results show strong fetal health classification performance under multiple privacy-preserving configurations, with synthetic augmentation and IFAT achieving up to 98% accuracy, an Average Precision (AP) score of 0.99, an F1-score of 0.98, and an ROC-AUC of 0.99, outperforming baseline methods. The proposed framework establishes a scalable, privacy-aware, and interpretable AI system for secure fetal health prediction in real-world healthcare systems.
