AB  - Existing efforts to combine large language models (LLMs) with reinforcement learning (RL) for autonomous driving remain limited by two issues: LLM-based planners typically lack enforceable safety guarantees and real-time feasibility, while RL controllers alone struggle with semantic generalization and interpretability. To overcome these limitations, we propose a hierarchical framework in which an LLM produces maneuver level plans and semantic safety priors, a hybrid mixture of RL experts executes context dependent control through a semantic gating mechanism and a constrained RL layer enforces temporal safety through adaptive shaping and shielding. To enable real time deployment we introduce a distillation procedure that aligns student policies with teacher trajectories using language-annotated explanations, yielding lightweight controllers that retain most of the teacher capability. The overall training paradigm follows a teacher–student structure: the teacher policy, which conditions on LLM-derived inputs, is trained through closed-loop constrained RL and subsequently generates annotated trajectory demonstrations. The student policy is then distilled from these demonstrations without invoking the LLM at inference time. LLM outputs take the form of structured discrete maneuver tokens (e.g., lane-keep, merge, yield) and numeric constraint parameters, and the LLM is queried at approximately 0.5 Hz during teacher training, fully decoupled from the 10 Hz control loop. Experiments on CARLA, SUMO and nuPlan demonstrate consistent gains in safety, rule compliance, task success and robustness under diverse scenarios, while achieving substantial reductions in inference latency and memory usage. The results show that coupling semantic planning with RL provides a coherent and practical foundation for interpretable, safe and efficient autonomous driving policies. The related code is available at https://github.com/liwenhao061/semantic-hierarchical-driving.
