AB  - Comprehensive assessment of earthquake-induced damage to indoor non-structural components (NSCs) is essential for evaluating post-earthquake building functionality. Existing computer vision methods mainly focus on single-task damage detection and often lack robustness in complex indoor environments. This study proposes Chat-NSDA, a tailored vision-language framework for automated post-earthquake NSC damage assessment. A domain-specific dataset, NSDA-caption, containing approximately 6830 indoor post-earthquake images across multiple functional spaces, NSC categories, and damage levels, was constructed for task-specific fine-tuning. Several state-of-the-art vision-language models were fine-tuned and evaluated, and the best-performing model was selected as the core inference engine. Chat-NSDA achieved 94.49% accuracy in NSC presence detection and 70.47% accuracy in damage-level classification, while generating structured and engineering-oriented damage descriptions. Applications to real indoor images from recent seismic events indicates its practical potential for rapid inspection and decision support. Compared with general-purpose VLMs, Chat-NSDA provides more structured and task-oriented NSC damage assessments in representative cases.
