KW  - Anomaly detection;Zero-shot;Semantic;Collaborative contrast
AB  - Zero-Shot Anomaly Detection is essential for open-world applications within industrial and medical domains. However, existing approaches based on Vision-Language models are generally constrained by two primary limitations: (1) a heavy reliance on exhaustive prompt engineering, which introduces significant empirical overhead and domain-specific bias; and (2) a susceptibility to noise interference from extensive background regions during feature contrast, which leads to computational inefficiency and high false-positive rates. To systematically address these challenges, we propose a novel framework termed the Semantic Filtering and Collaborative Contrast Network. The core principle of SFC-Net is to leverage textual semantics to “purify” the visual space. First, we replace complex prompt engineering with minimalist text prototypes to establish a robust semantic direction. Second, and most critically, we introduce a novel Semantic Filtering mechanism. Prior to batch-collaborative contrast, this mechanism utilizes text prototypes as probes to actively eliminate irrelevant background blocks. Finally, a semantic confidence gate is employed to intelligently fuse the visual contrast and semantic discrimination branches. Extensive experiments on multiple recognized benchmark datasets demonstrate that SFC-Net achieves state-of-the-art performance in both image-level classification and pixel-level localization under the zero-shot setting. The code involved in this study is publicly available on website https://github.com/SWLee777/SFC-NET.
