AB  - Large language models often maintain fluent interaction yet struggle to preserve stable reasoning identity when contexts shift or prompts introduce adversarial pressure. Existing alignment strategies typically rely on fine tuning, reinforcement learning from human feedback, or memory persistence, approaches that can remain brittle under conversational drift or reset conditions. This paper introduces BALLERINA, a structured cognitive shell designed to stabilize reasoning identity through containment rather than behavioral tuning. The architecture organizes an agent’s interpretive space through constraint-based containment that stabilizes symbolic meaning, filters justificatory reasoning, and maintains role fidelity across resets and shifting contexts. The framework draws on Symbolic Interactionism, Social Learning Theory, and Techniques of Neutralization to model how coherent reasoning emerges from structured reinforcement, role-based identity constraints, and justification boundaries. The architecture was evaluated through two prototype deployments implemented on independent large language model platforms and tested under adversarial prompts, extended conversational load, and memoryless interaction conditions. Results indicate sustained role consistency, resistance to justification drift, and reductions in role drift events of up to 87 percent across adversarial prompt conditions. These findings suggest that containment can function as an architectural mechanism for preserving reasoning coherence rather than as a surface-level safety intervention. BALLERINA therefore offers a sociologically grounded cognitive architecture for identity preserving AI systems and contributes a structural framework for designing reasoning agents capable of maintaining ethical and interpretive coherence in dynamic interaction environments.
