AB  - The integration of Internet of Things (IoT) architectures with software-defined wireless sensor networks (SDWSNs) has enhanced the capabilities and applications of these networks, such as smart transportation and healthcare monitoring systems. However, this integration also introduces attack surfaces that can be exploited for unauthorized access, data breaches, and malware propagation. Effective and intelligent intrusion detection and prevention systems (IDPS) have become essential to ensure the safety of IoT-based SDWSNs from attacks. By examining established and emerging AI paradigms, including generative adversarial networks (GANs), graph neural networks (GNNs), deep reinforcement learning (DRL), federated learning (FL), and the prospective role of security-oriented large language models (LLMs), this article provides a comprehensive review of new developments in AI-driven security mechanisms relevant to IoT-enabled SDWSNs and introduces a hierarchical security taxonomy organized according to primary security function, principal threat class, and AI/supporting security mechanism, while implementation context and evaluation criteria are considered separately. Substantial research gaps are identified, and future research directions are proposed that focus on developing AI methods to address unresolved security concerns. This review highlights promising AI-based approaches to enhance the security landscape for IoT and SDWSN applications, which represents a significant step forward toward providing robust, secure, and dependable network infrastructures.
