AB  - As large language models and agents continue to advance, their ability to automate software development tasks, particularly code generation, has garnered significant interest. One of the most widely used benchmarks for evaluating LLM performance in code generation is HumanEval, which focuses primarily on Python-based algorithmic problems. While useful for assessing Python code generation, HumanEval has limitations, particularly in evaluating statically typed languages such as Java, C, and C++, which require a deeper understanding of memory management, data structures, and low-level operations. Furthermore, these benchmarks tend to focus on function-level tasks, neglecting the complexity of repository-level tasks that involve designing complete systems with multiple modules, classes, and advanced programming concepts. It is worth noting that the C programming language, as the foundation for areas such as operating systems and embedded systems, has been under-explored in terms of code generation benchmarks. Considering the uniqueness of the C language, such as pointer manipulation, memory allocation, struct design, etc., the need for a comprehensive benchmark for C language code generation is evident. This paper proposes a new benchmark, i.e. RepoCBench, for the C language and verifies its effectiveness and importance through experiments. RepoCBench aims to support the study on C language generation of existing large language models and agents, and provide developers with valuable resources to tackle complex C development tasks, ultimately advancing the field of automated software development. Our experimental results demonstrate that Agents equipped with autonomous planning and tool-use capabilities substantially outperform standalone large language models in repository-level C code generation.
